WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Detailed maps of young lunar faults now give the clearest measure yet of how much the moon has contracted while cooling. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 224. Young tectonic landforms on the moon in a study reported by Faiz.org Space are being read as recent fault activity rather than older, worn-down features. The team maps scarps and related structures that formed as the moon lost heat and its crust adjusted. That approach yields tighter bounds on total radial contractions

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.560
than earlier global surveys. High-resolution orbital images separated fresh scarps from

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:40.660
ancient terrain. Latitude comparisons helped establish the timing of the most recent

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:45.360
shortening episodes, and crater counting on the scarps themselves supplies relative

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:51.840
ages that placed the youngest faults in geologically recent epochs. Contraction has persisted at a modest

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.940
rate into the present era rather than halting after the moon's early history, and the work

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.220
Mark refines models of thermal evolution and interior structure.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:05.960
Take a world about as wide as a modest continent, shedding internal heat for more than four

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.160
billion years, its outer shell slowly wrinkling under that long strain.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:15.480
On the moon, those wrinkles appear as networks of young fault scarps only a few meters high.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.020
Each one marks a moment when the crust shortened and buckled.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.700
The scarps remain sharp-edged against the older, impact-garden surface, so they stand out

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.860
clearly once you have the right topography. High resolution topography from orbiting spacecraft

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:35.400
captures those scarps at meter scales. Younger faults still display crisp slopes and almost

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:41.360
no erosion. Older ones have been softened by later bombardment. That contrast turns the lunar

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.260
surface into a geological clock for how the rate of radial shortening has changed through time.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.920
The method sidesteps the blurring of ancient heavily cratered terrain and focuses on structures

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:57.160
formed after the early heavy bombardment ended. The total amount of shortening remains modest

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:02.900
compared with the Moon's overall radius. Future missions carrying even finer resolution topographic

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:07.640
instruments could reveal whether the youngest scarps formed in clusters separated by long,

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:14.220
quiet intervals. That is the open question of a smooth cooldown versus pulses. NASA's Artemis

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.460
2 flight sent four astronauts around the Moon and brought them safely back to Earth.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:24.200
And Fies.org Space reports that post-flight analysis of the heat shield data is now complete.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:29.300
The review examined temperature, ablation, and structural integrity throughout reentry.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.340
Engineers compared recorded temperatures against pre-flight predictions.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:38.540
Structural integrity checks showed no unexpected degradation after the high speed return.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.480
The data set includes measurements from multiple sensors embedded in the shield material.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:49.080
Those readings matched model outputs with an acceptable margins across the entire entry profile,

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:54.400
and the results support continued use of the same shield design on future crude lunar flights.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:03:00.500
Higher mass stars assemble one family of planetary systems and lower mass stars assemble another,

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.620
according to new research described by Universe Today.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:09.320
The study used observed orbital architectures and stellar properties to separate the populations.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:13.540
The division appears tied to the mass of the central star at formation.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:18.900
Systems around more massive stars show different spacing and size patterns than those around

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:19.820
lower mass hosts.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:25.980
The separation emerges from statistical analysis of thousands of confirmed multi-planet systems.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:31.520
Orbital period ratios and planet radius distributions form the primary discriminance between

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:36.560
the two groups, and the split supplies a more detailed template for how exoplanet systems

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:37.280
are understood.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.600
An international research team has detected a rare form of methanol in space for the first

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.100
time as part of the Alma Compass large program, University Day reports.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:52.460
Alma's high-resolution spectra allowed the team to distinguish this isotopic variant

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:53.840
from more common methanol.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.120
Observations targeted regions where new stars and planets are assembling.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:04.220
Multiple spectral lines confirm the identification and ruled out contamination from terrestrial sources.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:09.529
The rare variant provides a fresh tracer for temperature and density conditions during

00:04:09.529 --> 00:04:10.589
molecule formation.

00:04:11.049 --> 00:04:16.269
The detection adds a new data point to inventories of complex organic molecules in star-forming

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:21.209
regions and expands the known chemical inventory available for planet-forming disks.

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:27.709
Cooling white dwarf models now predict a green star, a result space.com follows through refined

00:04:27.709 --> 00:04:28.889
atmospheric chemistry.

00:04:29.329 --> 00:04:33.889
The calculations indicate our own star could take on a green hue if it becomes a white

00:04:33.889 --> 00:04:38.709
dwarf after its death in billions of years, a late remnant some of the coverage calls

00:04:38.709 --> 00:04:44.229
a cosmic zombie. A green hue would appear under specific temperature and composition conditions,

00:04:44.489 --> 00:04:50.369
not previously modeled in detail. Refined atmospheric calculations now indicate the color arises

00:04:50.369 --> 00:04:55.209
when certain atomic transitions dominate the visible spectrum for a narrow temperature window.

00:04:55.869 --> 00:05:00.589
Earlier models had not accounted for the precise molecular interactions that could favor green

00:05:00.589 --> 00:05:05.489
wavelengths, including transitions that scatter or emit in the green part of the spectrum.

00:05:05.949 --> 00:05:10.489
The green phase would occur only after the white dwarf has cooled for an extended period,

00:05:10.969 --> 00:05:15.809
and the stage is brief compared with the remnants multi-billion-year cooling timeline, which

00:05:15.809 --> 00:05:17.069
makes it rare to observe.

00:05:17.629 --> 00:05:22.549
The same modeling framework suggests other transient colors could appear at different cooling stages,

00:05:23.049 --> 00:05:25.409
including Hughes' current surveys have yet to catch.

00:05:25.409 --> 00:05:32.349
Chandra X-ray images have been used to explain a radio formation nicknamed the treble clef, NASA reports,

00:05:32.869 --> 00:05:35.549
because its shape strongly resembles the musical symbol.

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:40.909
The X-ray data reveal the underlying cluster collision dynamics that produce the radio structure.

00:05:41.329 --> 00:05:46.129
The collision between galaxy clusters generates shocks and turbulence visible in X-rays,

00:05:46.609 --> 00:05:50.769
and those processes shape the radio emission into the distinctive clef-like pattern.

00:05:50.769 --> 00:05:55.509
The radio feature spans tens of millions of light years across the merged cluster.

00:05:56.209 --> 00:06:02.089
Chandra's imaging resolves temperature variations that trace the shock fronts responsible for accelerating particles.

00:06:02.529 --> 00:06:07.889
The match between X-ray morphology and radio contours supports the collision-driven origin model.

00:06:08.549 --> 00:06:15.629
Veonis has unveiled Atlas, a modular smart telescope system, and Space.com notes that pre-orders are now open.

00:06:15.629 --> 00:06:19.009
The design lets users observe with two optical tubes.

00:06:19.549 --> 00:06:24.089
Observers can swap or combine instruments without replacing the entire mount and electronics

00:06:24.089 --> 00:06:24.589
package.

00:06:25.089 --> 00:06:29.749
That approach aims to extend the usable life of the system as new sensors become available,

00:06:30.089 --> 00:06:34.249
so the instrument can be adapted to different wavelengths or resolutions over time.

00:06:34.829 --> 00:06:39.429
The modular architecture reduces the need to purchase entirely new hardware when technology

00:06:39.429 --> 00:06:39.909
advances.

00:06:39.909 --> 00:06:51.669
The dual tube configuration supports simultaneous imaging in separate bands or increased light-gathering power in a single band, and software updates can integrate new modules without hardware redesign.

00:06:52.529 --> 00:06:57.889
Later crude lunar flights are in line to fly the heat shield design that just cleared this post-flight review.

00:06:58.549 --> 00:07:06.529
Dual tube Atlas systems, once they reach observers, will be the first field test of how those swappable modules handle new sensors and wavelengths.

